<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Minstrel Boy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">published <a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;The Minstrel Boy&quot; is an Irish song by the poet Thomas Moore (1779–1852), published in 1813 in his Irish Melodies, a project that ran from 1808 to 1834. It is set to an air called &quot;The Moreen&quot;, which Moore said was traditional, but his publication of 1813 is the first record of the tune and the only source for that statement; the scholars who have studied his sources have traced no earlier one.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Charles Villiers Stanford named a source for the tune in a restored collection of Irish songs in 1895, but the scholars Veronica Ní Chinnéide and Una Hunt judge that he did not research it properly, and several of his attributions were corrected in Alfred Moffatt's Minstrelsy of Ireland in 1898. Hunt also found that Aloys Fleischmann cited as sources works that postdate Moore, and that Moore had better access to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and printed sources than earlier researchers believed. The air appears under Moore's title in George Thomson's collection of Scottish and Irish airs in 1816, which has led to the unproven suspicion that Thomson sent it to Moore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The song has two verses. A young minstrel goes to war with his father's sword and his harp; he falls, and rather than let the harp sound in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> under the foe's chain, he tears its strings apart. Like several of the Irish Melodies it is melancholy and nostalgic, and its harp, a romantic symbol of Ireland, is broken with no promise of restoration. Matthew Campbell has called its symbolism overdone and its tone histrionic beside The Last Rose of Summer, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-bernard-shaw">George Bernard Shaw</a> called the words visionary. Early American audiences took up its themes of freedom and passed over its references to slavery, though Moore, after travelling there, had written with disappointment of American slavery in 1806.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>It became one of the best-known songs of a collection that was immensely popular in Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, and Moore himself came to be nicknamed the Minstrel Boy. A third verse by an unknown author was added during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. Rafael María de Mendive's Spanish version of 1863, El trovador, kept the references to slaves at a time when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> still stood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>.</p><p>Composers and writers returned to it often: Stanford quoted it at the head of his Fourth Irish Rhapsody, John Philip Sousa worked it into his Mother Hubbard March of 1885, Benjamin Britten used distorted forms of it in his opera Owen Wingrave, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-joyce">James Joyce</a> parodied it in Finnegans Wake and quoted it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Ulysses</a>. Singers who recorded it include John McCormack, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a> and Joe Strummer, whose version was used in the film Black Hawk Down. It was played at the opening of the memorial at the World Trade Center site on 11 September 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Minstrel Boy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
